<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Prescott Joule</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Joule</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Prescott Joule was an English physicist from Salford who studied the nature of heat and discovered its relationship to mechanical work. The son of a wealthy brewer, he managed the family brewery and pursued science as a serious hobby, discovering Joule's first law in 1841. In 1843 he estimated the mechanical equivalent of heat, a direct challenge to the caloric theory, and his work led to the law of conservation of energy and the first law of thermodynamics. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-kelvin">Lord Kelvin</a> he developed an absolute temperature scale. He died at home in Sale, and the SI unit of energy, the joule, is named after him.</p></div>
